WEBVTT

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:19.320
Three years ago, my family and I moved to Cornwall to live the green life, and we ended up here at Newhouse Farm, where we built a water wheel to make electricity to light the house, become virtually self-sufficient in fruit and veg, but, unfortunately, not yet in wine.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:30.160
And I traded my gorgeous little sports car for a much more sensible agricultural vehicle that runs on biodiesel made from chip fat.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:35.160
We're back with a magazine programme, which means we've got something for everybody.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:42.240
Each week, rock chick and TV presenter Lauren Laverne will be investigating the posh end of the green market.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:45.640
I just hope they're not skimping on the luxury.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:52.480
And we'll be paying regular visits to the green end of the housing market.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:52.920
Hi, Dick, Jane and Chris here.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:56.680
We've got this Victorian house that we'd like to to eco renovate.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:01:00.760
And there'll be interviews with famous people, like Phil Tufnell.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:01.840
How are you getting home?

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:04.240
Um, plane!

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.239
It's a long way!

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:06.840
You live miles away!

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:12.640
And still time to show you what we're getting up to at Newhouse Farm.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:16.480
With plenty of top tips for a tiptop eco lifestyle.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:23.520
Here's a thought for you - it's not easy being green.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:37.080
Never has been, probably never will be, but it's an awful lot of fun trying.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:52.040
I know you lot are probably sitting shivering at home in front of the fire, but it's summertime here.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:08.200
That means you have to put up with my shorts and rather hairy legs while we crack on with our latest project - our very sexy PV panels.

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:24.480
Photovoltaic panels use sunlight to make electricity, and these little beauties are going to make us self sufficient in power.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:31.280
Master James Strawbridge has become quite the expert.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:39.000
This is a hybrid mono crystalline system, it's got 20% efficiency, and somehow, scientifically, the sun hits them and they make electricity.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:40.000
I think it's a bit of magic.

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:43.480
James did a history degree, but, look, it's that easy!

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:45.720
So light comes in here, it makes electricity, and what do we do with it?

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:49.560
Send it in the house and hopefully we'll get a year's supply from it.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:51.880
Interested?

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:56.680
Then you'll need a south facing roof or wall strong enough to support the panels.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:10.200
Very soon, no fossil fuels will be burned to make my cups of tea, which just as well seeing as I have so much entertaining to do over the series.

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:15.480
Each week, I'll be inviting a guest to take our eco test.

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:17.320
Victim number one is...

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:18.320
Phil!

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:24.040
I've come prepared.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:25.280
Got my boots on.

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:29.400
Do you know why you're here?

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:30.000
Well, not really.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:30.080
Good man, come on.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:34.480
'Phil Tufnell, but first up, a tour of the estate.'

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.160
I'm a little bit green.

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:37.800
I'm a little bit green.

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:45.040
Obviously, what we've done here is try to set up as much as we possibly can.

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:45.120
We're making our own electricity.

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:45.200
You're not into that sort of thing, are you?

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:47.360
Make you're own electricity, like a scientist!

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:48.240
Yeah!

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:48.920
Not quite.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.160
There you go, so what's up here?

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:56.480
You don't look as if you eat enough.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:58.960
No, well, I was a sportsman, you know?

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:05.440
We grow quite a lot of our own stuff.

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:06.480
Yes, yes.

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:09.160
Now, I know a couple of mates who grow their own stuff now, and they're kind of...

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:11.240
It's quite funny, isn't it?

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:20.000
These boys were cool kind of guys around town and now they all go down the allotment and start growing tomatoes, which is quite weird!

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.960
Have a bite of that.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:32.080
I know you like tomatoes.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:36.760
Does it need to be washed?

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:37.320
No, it doesn't!

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:37.920
There's nothing on it nasty.

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:42.560
That is a good tom, that is.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:42.760
It's not bad, is it?

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:43.320
No, no, it's a good tom.

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:49.160
With all these things growing we're making our wine and doing our stuff.

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:50.160
Do you know why?

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:51.280
Because we can.

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:51.600
We've got these little bad boys.

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:55.840
What are they?

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:59.280
These are ducks, and geese in this bit here.

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:02.560
We've got these in here because we eat the ducks, and we...

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:03.080
A l'orange.

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:04.920
And we eat their eggs, duck eggs, yes.

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:08.040
What's up with the faces?

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:08.440
Warts.

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:09.000
That's the way they're meant to be.

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:09.600
That's what they look like.

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:13.440
They're just the world's ugliest ducks.

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:14.240
They're ugly ducklings, aren't they?

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:19.040
They're genetically done like that so you don't feel bad about eating them!

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:19.280
This is something dear to your heart, I suppose.

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:24.680
Because you know about making biodiesel?

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:26.760
Is it like the chip shop?

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:27.240
Yes.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:31.960
Old chip oil that we turn into our diesel.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:34.040
How much are you paying for a litre?

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:34.960
I don't know!

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:37.360
£25 or something.

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:37.640
How much is a litre?

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:38.720
I don't know.

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:39.320
This is a worry if you don't even know how much it costs.

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:42.080
You fill your tank up and it's a hundred quid.

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:46.960
We make this for 14p a litre, which is quite...

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:48.160
Could I do this?

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:49.880
Could I go down my local?

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:52.400
Anybody can do this.

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:53.760
It looks quite complicated.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:54.360
Nah.

00:05:57.560 --> 00:05:59.760
We built that.

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:00.960
The toilet.

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:01.240
Yeah.

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:01.440
It's a compost loo.

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:03.480
Very nice.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:04.560
Curtain in there.

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:10.640
Will you come in?

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:11.320
The trepidation!

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:16.160
But, actually, it's a nice place to be.

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:17.240
It's all right.

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:22.720
When you're working in the garden, pull that up and it goes in...

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:29.000
There's a big cavity where you do it, and then you put some sawdust in it afterwards and at the end of it, we make compost.

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:29.440
All the poop turns into compost.

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:30.520
And you stick it on the veg.

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:33.840
I've just eaten that tomato, haven't I!

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:36.960
Have I just been eating your...

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:41.520
Didn't I tell you that?!

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:43.200
You ain't got one of these then?

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:43.280
No, I haven't got one of these.

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:45.680
I don't know whether the missus would be too happy with it, but I'll have to put it to her.

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:46.360
So we've got to the bottom of my green lifestyle.

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:55.800
Later in the show, I'll be asking Phil some difficult questions about his.

00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:01.760
But I can't spend all day hanging out with luvvies.

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:10.680
I've got man work to do on my PV panels.

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:16.000
That part of the roof is perfect for capturing sunshine because it points due south.

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:17.920
But that's the easy bit.

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:28.760
Now we have to take a cable from the control panel in the course barn, under this concrete, get all the way round to the back of the house, about 60 metres, and then we connect it to the National Grid.

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:38.360
But we don't want to dig up the concrete, because replacing it is one of the most environmentally unfriendly things you can do.

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:51.640
It's probably like an eighth of all the carbon footprint in the world comes from making concrete.

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:56.480
So we're using a mechanical mole to burrow under the yard.

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:05.880
The trouble, is I'm not sure where the wretched thing is.

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:08.840
What they're doing, if we're completely honest, we're trying to find it.

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:11.560
Here it comes!

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:11.720
All right, lads!

00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:18.120
That's moving quickly, isn't it?

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:21.840
You did that, sort of five metres across there in, what, less than ten minutes.

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:33.840
The mechanical mole team are all ready laying the cable.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:41.200
And we're making headway through the soft ground.

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:49.080
A few hours later, job done.

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:50.680
In fact, you want to know something, let's get a beer.

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:05.440
A hard day's work.

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:09.720
Luckily, we've got a top tip for sweaty chaps.

00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:14.280
James Strawbridge presents: an eco alternative to chemical deodorants.

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:24.480
Do you want to avoid getting unnecessary chemicals into your skin but still smell good?

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:27.400
Try Bauxite Crystal.

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:31.720
High performance eco control against body odour.

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:34.440
This crystal deposits salt on your skin, raising PH level, stopping bacteria in its tracks, as nature intended.

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:56.200
If you want to score, Rock Fresh gets results!

00:09:55.360 --> 00:09:56.560
Yeah!

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:56.840
Rock, Rock, Rock Fresh!

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:02.720
Joking apart, you really can buy these things at any hippy dippy store or website.

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:03.160
Go on, try one!

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:14.400
But first, here's Lauren Laverne to tackle one of life's great dilemmas well, for some.

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:20.880
You know those things that just don't come in the sentence as environmentally friendly or sustainable?

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:21.680
Things that don't even come in the colour green?

00:10:23.720 --> 00:10:28.600
But you really, really want one.

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:39.360
Looking for an eco friendly swimming pool is like trying to find an eco friendly nuclear bomb.

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:39.880
It's just not possible.

00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:49.200
But there are ways to make these works of the devil little bit less devil like.

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:58.120
It is possible to spend your way out of environmental embarrassment.

00:10:57.200 --> 00:11:11.160
What we've got in the background going on is a drilling rig drilling 100-metre deep holes for the geothermal heat pump that will provide all the energy to heat the house, heat the hot water and heat the swimming pool and swimming pool air.

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:11.960
There's a pipe in and out, what we call a closed loop.

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:17.320
We're taking the heat out of the earth and that's what being used to run the heat pump.

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:29.560
This is expensive to put in, but on a house of this size where we have a big house with a swimming pool, you're looking at a repayment over three or four years.

00:11:29.720 --> 00:11:33.120
They're doing their best, but they could do better.

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:52.160
Or you could have one like this, where nature does all the heating and bacteria eating for you, and there isn't even a muddy bottom.

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:55.400
So Will, what actually is this?

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:57.800
Is it a swimming pool, a pond, or something in between the two?

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:01.040
It's something in between the two.

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:03.400
It's a natural swimming pool.

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:16.720
This is the ultimate in enviro chic no chemicals to buy, and the little pump that swishes the water round only uses a quarter of the power needed for a regular pool.

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:28.360
A natural swimming pool pump only has to run while the plants are actually actively growing during the daytime.

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:28.840
So you've automatically halved the amount of time.

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:32.600
Six to eight hours is enough.

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:41.640
This is the carbonator which helps feed the plants, and it also means if the plants are healthy, they're giving oxygen to all the organisms that actually clean the water.

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:47.320
They're not just organisms they is bugs!

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:54.400
OK, Hal, so I understand you know a lot about the wildlife around this pool pond?

00:12:56.000 --> 00:12:59.480
Just found a water snail.

00:12:58.520 --> 00:12:59.760
Yes, look at that!

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:03.360
What about the snail poo, is that not a problem?

00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:03.680
That's not really a problem.

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:11.600
Feel like I'm shadowing a young David Bellamy here.

00:13:11.720 --> 00:13:14.680
There's a water boatman there.

00:13:14.080 --> 00:13:15.200
OK.

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:17.800
They just skate on the water.

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:21.960
And be assured, viewer, no bugs were harmed in the making of this film.

00:13:24.160 --> 00:13:25.520
It's an animal friendly skimmer.

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:33.880
Any animals or bugs that get caught in it can either swim back at night or climb out the back safely, and get back into the pond overland.

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:42.600
I think I'd be quite squeamish about bugs and insects.

00:13:41.720 --> 00:13:46.520
Are there are a lot of those little critters in there if I was going to swim in there?

00:13:45.960 --> 00:13:55.120
Most of the larger species keep to the edges, in the plants where the main habitat is, but there is smaller microscopic bugs through the swimming area.

00:13:54.960 --> 00:14:00.600
These actually clean the water, so they're an important part of it.

00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:00.800
So how clean?

00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:00.960
Can you show us?

00:14:00.480 --> 00:14:07.280
This water we tested recently, two months ago, and it was drinking water quality, so it's fine to drink.

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:11.800
Really?

00:14:10.840 --> 00:14:12.000
Can you...?

00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:15.120
In fact, the water tastes quite sweet.

00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:17.360
It's...

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:17.640
Delicious.

00:14:16.720 --> 00:14:18.000
Would you like some?

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:18.320
OK, go on, then.

00:14:22.280 --> 00:14:24.440
That is actually really nice!

00:14:23.480 --> 00:14:24.920
It's lovely, yeah.

00:14:26.520 --> 00:14:31.720
A top of the range pool like this will set you back over £100,000.

00:14:31.880 --> 00:14:39.640
All swimming pools are expensive, but at least these don't cost the earth, and you can invite your friends over and watch them go green with envy.

00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:39.880
OK...

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:48.600
Dry clean only, but... fine.

00:14:52.160 --> 00:14:55.400
It's a bit like the Lost Boys in here.

00:14:58.280 --> 00:15:04.560
Up next: Our first visit of many to Chris Mead and Jaye Hayter.

00:15:04.840 --> 00:15:10.360
Hi, Dick, we've got this Victorian house that we'd like to eco renovate.

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:19.720
We're looking at wall insulation, loft insulation, green ideas for solar energy, alternative energy, wind power, wind turbine.

00:15:19.280 --> 00:15:25.120
Any of your magnificent inventions can come along that would fit snugly into a Victorian house.

00:15:24.240 --> 00:15:25.800
That's what we'd be interested in.

00:15:25.880 --> 00:15:28.840
'Flattery will get you everywhere.'

00:15:51.360 --> 00:15:54.440
Let's go into the lounge.

00:15:56.800 --> 00:15:59.480
This is the lounge.

00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:02.920
This is our front room of our...

00:16:01.960 --> 00:16:03.280
So, this is the lounge!

00:16:02.440 --> 00:16:04.680
It will be!

00:16:03.800 --> 00:16:05.800
Where's the sofa?

00:16:05.240 --> 00:16:07.960
Look at the floorboards.

00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:08.480
Are you going to keep them?

00:16:07.720 --> 00:16:12.120
Yes, we will sand them down, keep them as original, but they're very good solid floors.

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:22.800
Chris and Jaye have £100,000 of their hard earned cash to green up this wreck of a house in London.

00:16:26.400 --> 00:16:32.960
They want it to be an all singing, all dancing temple to green technology.

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:34.960
There's a huge amount to be done.

00:16:36.480 --> 00:16:37.560
We're talking blank canvasses.

00:16:36.760 --> 00:16:39.400
Can you do almost anything.

00:16:38.640 --> 00:16:41.440
'There's damp in the basement.'

00:16:40.600 --> 00:16:47.000
Used to have air bricks, but over a period of time, they've all been blocked up, and it's starting to smell.

00:16:46.160 --> 00:16:51.920
The whole house needs rewiring and replumbing, and so does the garden.

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:53.640
I want chickens out there!

00:16:52.720 --> 00:16:54.720
I want free range eggs.

00:16:57.360 --> 00:17:01.880
'And this will be the control centre for all the green technology.'

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:09.119
Hopefully, this is going to be an eco comms room.

00:17:08.160 --> 00:17:09.280
Not yet!

00:17:09.160 --> 00:17:19.880
Their hundred grand has to go a long way to create a bijou residence, complete with solar power and state of the art heating.

00:17:21.680 --> 00:17:30.560
Thinking about your priorities, if you've got the idea you want solar PV and under floor heating, but perhaps if we look around, insulation might be the first thing you need to focus on.

00:17:31.360 --> 00:17:36.040
It's very easy to think, "let's go green, let's buy solar panels".

00:17:35.400 --> 00:17:38.600
James is absolutely right.

00:17:37.760 --> 00:17:43.960
Lot of insulation is the best way to start, and could cut heating bills by £200 a year or more.

00:17:43.120 --> 00:17:46.800
And this is my favourite kind.

00:17:46.120 --> 00:17:50.040
Sheep's wool.

00:17:49.240 --> 00:17:50.400
This isn't cheap.

00:17:49.480 --> 00:17:53.560
Any insulation is good insulation, and this is green, so it's good.

00:17:53.240 --> 00:17:58.360
Sheep's wool costs four times as much as the cheapest insulation on the market.

00:17:57.600 --> 00:18:01.240
But Jaye thinks she's worth it.

00:18:00.280 --> 00:18:01.840
This is brilliant.

00:18:01.920 --> 00:18:07.560
You can put it up, you're not given any fibres, manmade fibres on your hands, you can take it down, it's malleable.

00:18:06.640 --> 00:18:08.160
It's brilliant.

00:18:07.280 --> 00:18:10.480
It's naturally fire retardant.

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:13.200
It's got to be laid properly into the edges.

00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:13.920
That's where James comes in.

00:18:13.040 --> 00:18:18.920
It's important to get it into the eaves, because all those awkward little corners that I can get in that my dad can't... !

00:18:19.680 --> 00:18:26.600
It's really important, because if you've got one gap, then it's almost like all this is worthless.

00:18:35.960 --> 00:18:43.520
Our houses are responsible for a quarter of the carbon emissions in the UK.

00:18:42.760 --> 00:18:46.080
Half of the heat loss in a typical home is through the roof and loft.

00:18:53.920 --> 00:19:03.720
We're having to put three layers of insulation up because you need about 270mm, a foot in old money, of insulation in your roof.

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:10.840
No good saying you've got insulation with four inches of fibre glass The more you put up, the more heat you keep in, which means the less energy you use every winter when you're heating your house.

00:19:10.480 --> 00:19:16.080
So, this is a good investment, and you only ever do it once because get enough in there, and you'll stay warm.

00:19:16.360 --> 00:19:20.320
Over the next six months, this place is going to be a hive of activity.

00:19:19.480 --> 00:19:21.440
From the top of the roof to the bottom of the garden.

00:19:25.120 --> 00:19:27.880
And don't forget, they want chickens!

00:19:28.160 --> 00:19:32.840
It does.

00:19:33.720 --> 00:19:36.960
We'll be with them every step of the way.

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:37.280
OK, eurgh!

00:19:36.360 --> 00:19:37.640
Done!

00:19:36.720 --> 00:19:38.040
Yeah!

00:19:50.320 --> 00:19:51.480
Nice and warm, it feels good.

00:19:50.520 --> 00:19:53.720
They've got a long way to go.

00:19:52.760 --> 00:20:00.000
I hope they know what they've let themselves in for, because we've been here for three years, and we keep finding new things to do.

00:19:59.040 --> 00:20:03.280
And most of them are aimed at trying to save us money.

00:20:02.680 --> 00:20:06.680
Our PV panels are no exception.

00:20:05.760 --> 00:20:07.040
Jim!

00:20:12.160 --> 00:20:14.040
How much electricity today?

00:20:13.080 --> 00:20:17.080
Look how much!

00:20:16.120 --> 00:20:17.440
Only Jim's going to have something like that in his pocket.

00:20:20.680 --> 00:20:23.400
300 watts per square metre today.

00:20:23.560 --> 00:20:29.080
We don't want days like this, we want sunshine, we want it to pay for itself, have a bit of an income.

00:20:29.800 --> 00:20:32.200
'Because this job ain't cheap.'

00:20:39.680 --> 00:20:40.840
It's all right, one person's allowed to do that to me!

00:20:39.880 --> 00:20:41.520
How much does it cost, mate?

00:20:41.920 --> 00:20:48.520
This particular system is going to cost about £15,000, which is a bargain!

00:20:49.360 --> 00:20:51.760
He said that with a straight face!

00:20:51.400 --> 00:21:04.960
We're paying £15,000 for our insulation, where we get £2,500 grant, so, for £12,500, we're probably getting enough photovoltaics to keep us in electricity for a very long time.

00:21:06.960 --> 00:21:09.120
Ooh, the last one!

00:21:08.360 --> 00:21:11.240
If we were to connect this to that wire, we'd be making electricity.

00:21:10.640 --> 00:21:13.200
We would, but we'd give Jim a bit of a shock.

00:21:12.440 --> 00:21:15.320
Because he's connecting the wire.

00:21:14.480 --> 00:21:16.520
All right, which one do we put it in?!

00:21:19.480 --> 00:21:28.320
Bearing in mind you could easily spend about £1,000 a year on electricity for a house like ours, the panels will pay for themselves eventually.

00:21:28.920 --> 00:21:30.240
What are you doing?

00:21:30.720 --> 00:21:34.400
Waiting for the sun!

00:21:35.400 --> 00:21:41.920
With every bit of kit we install here at Newhouse Farm, our carbon footprint is getting smaller and smaller, but how do we compare with other people?

00:21:41.080 --> 00:21:44.120
There's one way to find out.

00:21:45.880 --> 00:21:53.680
Each week, we'll be whipping out our ecological calculator to put a guest to the eco test.

00:21:55.560 --> 00:21:58.760
Of all the calculators on the Web, we've picked this one by the WWF.

00:21:58.040 --> 00:22:04.120
It's fairly quick to do, that's telly for you, and gives a clear and simple picture of your impact on the world.

00:22:03.240 --> 00:22:11.880
By answering a series of questions, we'll get a measure of how many planets would be needed to support life if everyone in the world lived the same way as them.

00:22:11.640 --> 00:22:23.120
I was a bit peeved by my score of 2.4 planets, but this calculator isn't designed for somebody who's all ready doing lots of green projects, it's designed for normal people like Phil Tufnell.

00:22:23.240 --> 00:22:30.760
I've got some simple questions, and this gives a flavour for how you use the energy and resources in your life.

00:22:30.560 --> 00:22:31.840
Righto.

00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:32.840
How would you describe your diet?

00:22:32.720 --> 00:22:35.120
All round, love everything.

00:22:34.240 --> 00:22:35.520
Meat?

00:22:34.640 --> 00:22:36.400
Yes.

00:22:35.480 --> 00:22:36.960
You're not a vegetarian, are you, mate?

00:22:36.160 --> 00:22:38.600
No, I'm not.

00:22:37.920 --> 00:22:40.720
Fish, everything, veggies, lovely.

00:22:41.360 --> 00:22:42.520
Right.

00:22:41.640 --> 00:22:47.280
In the last 12 months, have you bought any of the following: television or a hi-fi?

00:22:46.680 --> 00:22:49.640
Yes.

00:22:48.960 --> 00:22:50.640
The answer is supposed to be no, to be brief!

00:22:52.200 --> 00:22:53.320
Oh, is it?

00:22:52.360 --> 00:22:54.560
I was feeling all proud!

00:22:53.600 --> 00:22:57.160
I bought a great big telly!

00:22:56.200 --> 00:22:57.760
You said yes and you were so pleased with yourself!

00:22:56.840 --> 00:22:58.280
Good man, OK.

00:22:57.400 --> 00:22:58.560
There we go.

00:23:01.720 --> 00:23:07.600
I quite like this question: What kind of vehicle do you travel in?

00:23:06.880 --> 00:23:17.440
This is where I fall down, and I will drive up to the paper shop to get me paper instead of having a walk or getting on a bike.

00:23:16.520 --> 00:23:21.080
I'd say naughty boy on that one.

00:23:21.320 --> 00:23:22.960
Which of these best describes the vehicle you use most?

00:23:22.040 --> 00:23:23.320
Oh dear.

00:23:22.360 --> 00:23:23.680
Sodding large!

00:23:22.720 --> 00:23:23.920
Yeah.

00:23:26.400 --> 00:23:29.680
It's 4.4.

00:23:30.120 --> 00:23:33.120
You've got to have a bit of fun being green as well.

00:23:32.200 --> 00:23:35.280
You've got to have a bit of a laugh, haven't you?

00:23:34.960 --> 00:23:38.680
There's nothing green about having a 4.4 litre petrol car.

00:23:37.880 --> 00:23:40.600
I feel very ashamed and I'm going to get rid of it.

00:23:40.160 --> 00:23:46.840
I still need a big car and a 4x4 because I live out in the country a little bit, but, as you say, 4.4.

00:23:45.960 --> 00:23:47.920
You say a little bit, you say you live out the country and then you add a little bit?

00:23:49.840 --> 00:23:50.960
Well, no, a little bit.

00:23:50.720 --> 00:23:53.720
So you need a 4x4.

00:23:52.760 --> 00:23:59.480
You've got lanes and little tracks as well that you have to go down, so you can't be doing it in a Mini, mate!

00:23:58.720 --> 00:24:00.320
It's not working on me!

00:23:59.560 --> 00:24:03.120
I'm not feeling the mud!

00:24:02.200 --> 00:24:05.920
All right, OK, I'll sell it tomorrow!

00:24:05.520 --> 00:24:07.960
How do you heat your home?

00:24:07.000 --> 00:24:08.200
Erm...

00:24:07.720 --> 00:24:08.880
Gas central heating.

00:24:08.000 --> 00:24:10.480
Gas?

00:24:09.600 --> 00:24:11.360
Yes.

00:24:10.400 --> 00:24:11.680
Boiler, radiators.

00:24:12.520 --> 00:24:17.880
Do you buy your electricity on a green tariff?

00:24:16.920 --> 00:24:18.000
Erm...

00:24:17.720 --> 00:24:22.480
You don't even know what you pay for your electricity!

00:24:21.520 --> 00:24:22.840
What is a green tariff?

00:24:21.880 --> 00:24:27.640
You can buy green electricity, pay the same price as you pay for ordinary electricity.

00:24:28.480 --> 00:24:33.640
And they don't have to put a great big hose...

00:24:32.680 --> 00:24:35.640
They don't do anything.

00:24:34.720 --> 00:24:36.680
It's just green electricity?

00:24:35.760 --> 00:24:36.960
They don't do anything.

00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:37.600
You just buy renewable.

00:24:36.760 --> 00:24:40.520
How warm do you keep your home?

00:24:39.760 --> 00:24:44.560
Cool, comfortable, warm or hot?

00:24:43.720 --> 00:24:44.920
I would say comfortable.

00:24:43.960 --> 00:24:46.720
Which is 14 to 17 degrees.

00:24:47.200 --> 00:24:48.560
That's...

00:24:48.280 --> 00:24:49.640
Oh, yes...

00:24:48.840 --> 00:24:53.520
I thought it would have been about 19.

00:24:52.760 --> 00:24:54.240
No, 19 is warm.

00:24:54.040 --> 00:24:55.200
Is it?

00:24:54.240 --> 00:24:55.400
OK.

00:24:54.440 --> 00:24:55.560
Yeah.

00:24:54.720 --> 00:24:57.280
I don't have the heating on in the summer.

00:24:58.600 --> 00:25:00.000
Really, Phil?

00:24:59.160 --> 00:25:00.480
You don't just think that's common sense?

00:25:04.720 --> 00:25:07.720
No, but I'd keep it normal, comfortable.

00:25:09.440 --> 00:25:10.600
Comfortable for you.

00:25:09.680 --> 00:25:17.840
Your footprint, to support you, if everybody in the UK lived like you, we'd actually need 3.97 planets.

00:25:17.840 --> 00:25:19.440
Is that really bad?

00:25:19.480 --> 00:25:21.440
It's quite a lot of planets, mate.

00:25:21.520 --> 00:25:22.640
Yeah.

00:25:21.680 --> 00:25:22.960
It means that you're sort of that's a lot of planets being used up.

00:25:29.720 --> 00:25:33.600
How are you getting home?

00:25:32.640 --> 00:25:33.920
Erm...

00:25:34.560 --> 00:25:36.360
Plane.

00:25:36.560 --> 00:25:39.200
It's a long way!

00:25:38.240 --> 00:25:39.480
You live miles away!

00:25:40.000 --> 00:25:43.120
A plane?!

00:25:42.400 --> 00:25:43.880
Hold on!

00:25:42.920 --> 00:25:47.040
I in live England.

00:25:46.360 --> 00:25:50.720
It may be Cornwall and, yes, but don't hide!

00:25:49.800 --> 00:25:51.640
I'm getting a plane.

00:25:50.800 --> 00:25:54.560
But it runs on chip fat!

00:25:53.680 --> 00:26:01.200
It's a long way back to Surrey on a horse and cart.

00:26:00.240 --> 00:26:01.680
It will make me about three weeks.

00:26:00.720 --> 00:26:05.440
Mr Tufnell, you're the first guest to take the eco test.

00:26:04.640 --> 00:26:07.600
It makes me feel a bit better about my score.

00:26:08.440 --> 00:26:11.520
It's not easy being green!

00:26:11.080 --> 00:26:12.960
It's not that difficult!

00:26:23.560 --> 00:26:34.840
On a good day, we should be making more electricity than we need, but here's the thing: we can sell that power back to the National Grid.

00:26:33.880 --> 00:26:35.200
Ready, Andy?

00:26:34.240 --> 00:26:35.440
We're ready.

00:26:36.680 --> 00:26:47.160
And even better, next year, the Government are going to double the price paid for surplus power, which will reduce my payback by about five years.

00:26:46.280 --> 00:26:50.080
If you want to know more, check out the website at: Come on!

00:26:57.400 --> 00:27:02.040
This great gadget tells me how much electricity is being made right now.

00:27:01.080 --> 00:27:02.480
It's not the sun, it's the machine.

00:27:04.600 --> 00:27:06.960
It takes three minutes to warm up.

00:27:06.040 --> 00:27:09.880
So take a look at what's coming up next week.

00:27:11.280 --> 00:27:14.520
Lauren gets to grips with furniture recycling.

00:27:14.080 --> 00:27:17.840
We could buy that plain Victorian beach chair.

00:27:18.120 --> 00:27:19.360
How much do you think?

00:27:18.400 --> 00:27:21.440
£100.

00:27:21.240 --> 00:27:22.360
£3.

00:27:21.400 --> 00:27:22.800
No!

00:27:22.240 --> 00:27:23.800
£3?!

00:27:24.520 --> 00:27:30.000
And in London, James persuades Bridget to join her for a spot of gardening.

00:27:31.240 --> 00:27:33.000
Look at the snails!

00:27:33.440 --> 00:27:35.080
Kill 'em.

00:27:34.240 --> 00:27:35.960
No!

00:27:39.880 --> 00:27:46.280
And we examine the plight of the honeybee with rock star Alex James.

00:27:45.400 --> 00:27:46.560
Well, Martin, to be or not to be, that is the question!

00:27:45.640 --> 00:27:47.080
What's the answer?

00:27:54.720 --> 00:27:55.960
Look at that.

00:27:55.080 --> 00:27:56.560
'Now, buzz off!'

00:27:55.600 --> 00:27:56.760
Yes!

00:27:56.120 --> 00:27:59.480
2,331!

00:28:01.720 --> 00:28:08.240
That gorgeous number, in fact, enough to power the house right now, which means...

00:28:07.440 --> 00:28:09.080
That's enough for tea!

00:28:08.120 --> 00:28:09.440
Get the kettle on!
